  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 317 AM EDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\nAn upper-level ridge and stalled out frontal boundary remain the \nprimary items of focus for this forecast period. Hot and humid air \nprevails once again this afternoon as heat indicies are forecast to \nbe around 100. The heat advisory was expanded to the entire forecast \narea. An overall absence of convection across WI and MI have limited \nthe anticipated cloud cover, thus, a hotter afternoon than \noriginally forecast. We'll run into a similar situation for Friday, \nwhich, as it stands right now, is heat-advisory free, but may need \nto be reevaluated based on convective trends. \n\nTurning now to those pesky thunderstorm chances; initial 00z CAM \nruns revealed dreadful initialization with over-convection noted in \nIA and WI at the time, while struggling to handle convection \nelsewhere in the Plains. This weakens already battered forecast \nconfidence in this pattern. However, sifting past the over-\nconvection, ensembles and hourly runs appear to be converging on \nstorms developing over southern WI this evening in the vicinity of a \nstrengthening 500-mb jet and stalled front below. Timing is murky, \nbut \"after dark\" appears to be a reasonable bet locally. The overall \nsevere weather environment is favorable, but nothing truly alarming, \nwith ample MUCAPE, bulk shear near 30 knots and mid-level lapse \nrates near 7 C/km. Damaging wind gusts and hail appear to be the \nmost likely outcome from storm clusters that emerge from southern \nWisconsin. This activity may persist beyond daybreak Friday \ndepending on the upstream start time. \n\nWith the upper-level ridge still folding over our area Friday and \nSaturday, thunderstorm chances persist but details are sparse owing \nto the anticipated feedback loop day-to-day. Perhaps some relief \nSunday and the start of Monday as Canadian high pressure seeping \nover the eastern Great Lakes works to suppress the upper-level ridge \nwestward for a time. Guidance is split on how the coming week \nevolves, but the overall upper air pattern to me favors a upper-\nlevel ridge becoming centered over the mid-Mississippi Valley as a \ntrough arrives to the Pacific Northwest. This would promote \ncontinued heat and storm chances.",
